{"purpose":"Record that `eka status` reports SUPERSEDED container instances as active, so its `active:` lines disagree with the canonical highest-instance container state that the projections show.","content":"`repoActiveContainers` in `eka-cli/cmd/status.go` (~L290-304) iterates EVERY unit of the project and appends any whose `stateVector.containerState == \"active\"`, WITHOUT deduping by ctr line or taking the highest instance (ADR-025). When a container line has an older active instance superseded by a newer instance (revised, or completed), the stale active instance lingers in the store and is still printed as `active:`. Observed during EKA v2 M6 orchestration: store current state = `ctr:hardening-roadmap` completed, `ctr:solo-author` planned, yet `eka status` printed both as `active:`. `eka view`/`eka watch` (view/activeContainerUnits, highest-instance per line) correctly showed no active container. Fix: resolve to the highest instance per ctr line before filtering to active, matching the projection engine.","investigationSummary":"Found during EKA v2 M6 orchestration while diagnosing an empty `eka watch wave` board. Verified against the store (ctr:solo-author instances v1=active / v2=planned; ctr:hardening-roadmap v1=active / v2=completed) and read `cmd/status.go:290-304`. The projection path (`eka-core/view/graph.go` activeContainerUnits) applies ADR-025; the status path does not.","conclusion":"Real correctness bug in the `eka status` execution probe: it can claim a container is active when the canonical state is not. Impact: user trust and any automation keying on `status`. Fix is small (highest-instance resolution) and should be covered by a test with a superseded-active fixture."}